ClassiCube for RISC OS
======================

ClassiCube is a free, open source game compatible with Minecraft
Classic: build and explore in single player, or join the community's
servers. https://www.classicube.net

This is a port to RISC OS 5 on ARMv7 (Raspberry Pi 2 and later, and
other Cortex-A boards with VFP). It draws with riscos-mesa's OpenGL
(in software) through SDL 2.

Needs
-----
- RISC OS 5.28 or later (for the AcornSSL module, used for downloads).
- SharedUnixLibrary 1.16+ and ARMEABISupport, from PackMan.
- For sound: SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager (ssb.zip from
  https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html), or
  DigitalRenderer.

First run
---------
The launcher offers to download the game's sounds, music and textures
(a few MB). AcornSSL may ask whether to trust a server's certificate;
choose Accept, or Accept always.

Your options, worlds, screenshots and texture packs are kept in
<Choices$Write>.ClassiCube (set ClassiCube$Data before running to
use somewhere else).

Speed
-----
Drawing is done by the CPU (riscos-mesa). The game draws at 640x360 and
the picture is stretched to the window or the screen; on a Raspberry Pi
with the VideoOverlay module the display does the stretching. To change
it, set before running (or edit !Run):
  ClassiCube$RenderSize   e.g. 480x270 (faster) or 854x480 (sharper),
                          or full (the window's own size: slowest)
  ClassiCube$Overlay      0 to stretch without the overlay
  ClassiCube$WindowSize   the window's size, e.g. 1600x900
With the VideoOverlay module (Raspberry Pi), the window opens at 3x, 2x
or 1.5x the drawing size, the biggest that fits on the desktop (1280x720
on a 1920x1080 desktop): the display stretches the picture, so a bigger
window costs no speed. Without an overlay the CPU stretches it, so the
window stays 854x480. The title bar shows which: [overlay], [plotted],
or [overlay hidden] while a menu or window is over the game. If it says
[plotted] on a Pi, *Show EGL$OverlayStatus says why (overlays are off
while RDPServer has a client connected, and in full screen). The
/client resolution command sets a size to keep.
In the game's options: a shorter view distance (default 128 here) helps
on big server maps; smooth lighting and fancy effects cost speed.

Keys
----
A window with the keys and mouse buttons opens when ClassiCube starts.
Untick "Show this when ClassiCube starts" to stop it; it's also under
Settings > Keys and mouse in the launcher.

Mouse
-----
While you play, the game holds the pointer so the mouse turns the view
(the cross in the middle is your aim); the title bar says "Escape frees
the mouse". Press Escape for the pause menu and the pointer, and "Back
to game" to carry on. The game also lets go (and pauses) when another
window takes the input focus.

Problems
--------
Messages are written to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.ClassiCubeLog (and OpenAL's to
<Wimp$ScrapDir>.ClassiCubeAL).

